Completion theorem: the ring-buffer equational theory admits a complete rewrite
system, verified by Knuth-Bendix completion (std-only Rust).

Signature: single sort; unary operations {push, pop}; variables x, y, ...
Initial rules (equations oriented left->right):
  push(pop(x)) -> x
  pop(push(x)) -> x

Completion pass 1: 4 critical pair(s) considered
  overlap push(pop(x)): joins (normal form x)
  overlap pop(push(pop(x))): joins (normal form pop(x))
  overlap push(pop(push(x))): joins (normal form push(x))
  overlap pop(push(x)): joins (normal form x)
Completion converged: all critical pairs join, no new rules needed.

Final rule set (2 rule(s)):
  push(pop(x)) -> x
  pop(push(x)) -> x

Check (a) - termination: every rule strictly decreases the size-lex order
  push(pop(x)) -> x (size 2 -> size 0): decreases
  pop(push(x)) -> x (size 2 -> size 0): decreases
  (a) terminating: PASS

Check (b) - local confluence: every critical pair joins
  overlap push(pop(x)): nf(inner) = x == x = nf(outer): join
  overlap pop(push(pop(x))): nf(inner) = pop(x) == pop(x) = nf(outer): join
  overlap push(pop(push(x))): nf(inner) = push(x) == push(x) = nf(outer): join
  overlap pop(push(x)): nf(inner) = x == x = nf(outer): join
  (b) all 4 critical pair(s) join: PASS

Check (c) - the original two rules are present
  push(pop(x)) -> x : present
  pop(push(x)) -> x : present
  (c) original rules present: PASS

Check (d) - the system decides the theory on samples
  normalize(push(pop(push(x)))) == normalize(push(x)): push(x) == push(x): PASS
  normalize(pop(push(pop(x)))) == normalize(pop(x)): pop(x) == pop(x): PASS
  normalize(push(push(pop(pop(x))))) == normalize(x): x == x: PASS
  normalize(push(pop(pop(push(x))))) == normalize(pop(push(x))): x == x: PASS
  normalize(push(pop(push(pop(x))))) == normalize(x): x == x: PASS
  normalize(push(x)) != normalize(pop(x)): push(x) != pop(x): PASS
  (d) samples decided correctly: PASS

Overall: PASS

Explanation:
  The rules push(pop(x)) -> x and pop(push(x)) -> x say that push and pop are
  mutual inverses. The only overlaps of the two LHSs are the unary terms
  push(pop(push(x))) and pop(push(pop(x))): in both cases the two ways of
  rewriting coincide (both sides normalize to push(x) resp. pop(x)), so every
  critical pair joins and completion adds no new rule. Each rule strictly
  decreases the size-lexicographic order (size 2 -> size 0), so the system is
  terminating; by Newman's lemma termination + local confluence = confluence,
  i.e. the system is complete. Normal forms are the alternating-free stacks
  x, push(x), push(push(x)), ..., pop(x), pop(pop(x)), ..., and two terms are
  equal in the theory iff their normal forms coincide, so the system decides
  the ring-buffer equational theory.
